@page "/privacy"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

@* BUD-152. The wording is the draft on Confluence (Data Protection (UK GDPR) › Privacy Notice), awaiting review. Change it
   there first, then here, and bump LegalDocuments.PrivacyVersion. *@

<PageTitle>Privacy · Home Budget</PageTitle>

<LegalPage Title="Privacy notice" Version="@LegalDocuments.PrivacyVersion" Other="terms of use" OtherHref="@LegalDocuments.TermsPath">
    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p>
        Home Budget is run by [operator's legal name] ("we"), [address]. We're the controller of the personal information described
        here. If you have a question or a request about your information, contact us at [privacy email address].
    </p>

    <h2>What this covers</h2>
    <p>
        What information Home Budget holds about you and the other people in your household, why, who else is involved, how long
        it's kept, and your rights.
    </p>

    <h2>What we hold</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Your account:</strong> your name and email address, as given to us by Microsoft or Google when you sign in; which
            sign-in you use; the households you belong to and your role in each; and your settings.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your household's budget:</strong> what you and other members enter. That includes the household's name; the
            people in it; their income, outgoings, accounts and pots, and transfers; amounts, dates, schedules and notes; and the
            history of changes, including who made them.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Invitations:</strong> the email address someone was invited with, while the invitation is open.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Recent activity:</strong> a list of recent changes in a household and who made them, so members can see what's
            changed. It's kept for 3 months.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Technical information:</strong> the date and time of each request, the page or action, how long it took, and any
            errors. These records are kept to keep the app working and secure. They don't contain names, email addresses, amounts or
            anything you've typed.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        We don't hold passwords: Microsoft or Google checks who you are. Please don't enter card numbers, passwords or other
        sensitive details in notes.
    </p>

    <h2>People who don't sign in</h2>
    <p>
        A household can record details about people who don't have an account, such as a partner or a child with a part-time job.
        Usually that's a first name and the income or outgoings that are theirs. The household member who adds them should let them
        know. They have the same rights as anyone else here. They can ask us, or the household, to correct or remove their details.
    </p>

    <h2>Why we use it, and our lawful basis</h2>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What for</th><th>Lawful basis</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Providing Home Budget: your account, your households, and the budget you keep in them</td><td>Contract: it's needed to provide the service you've asked for</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Keeping the app secure and working: technical records, limits on how often requests can be made, and security scans</td><td>Legitimate interests: protecting you, other users and the service</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Details of people who don't sign in, added by a household</td><td>[Legitimate interests. To confirm.]</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Answering your requests and complaints, and keeping a record of them</td><td>Legal obligation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>We don't sell your information, use it for advertising, or make decisions about you by automated means.</p>

    <h2>Who else is involved</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Microsoft Azure</strong> hosts the app and its database, and stores the technical records, all in the UK (UK South).</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Microsoft</strong> or <strong>Google</strong>, when you choose to sign in with them. They tell us your name and
            email address. Their own privacy notices cover what they do when you sign in.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>They act on our instructions under contracts that protect your information.</p>

    <h2>Who can see your household's budget</h2>
    <p>
        The members of a household can see its budget, according to their role (Viewer, User or Admin). Admins can invite people,
        change roles and remove members. Nobody outside the household can see it. We only look at a household's data when we need
        to: to fix a problem you've reported, or when the law requires it.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Information</th><th>Kept for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Your account</td><td>Until you delete it [or until it's been unused for [period], after a warning]</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A household's budget</td><td>Until the household is deleted, or until its last member deletes their account</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Recent activity</td><td>3 months</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Technical records</td><td>30 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Database backups</td><td>[period]. Deleted information leaves the backups when they expire.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Requests and complaints</td><td>[period]</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>When you leave a household or delete your account</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Leaving a household:</strong> you lose access. What's recorded in the household stays, because it's the household's
            shared record of its money. You can download a copy first.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Deleting your account</strong> (Settings): your account, sign-in and settings are deleted straight away, and you
            leave every household. Households only you are in are deleted with everything in them. Households you own that others use
            pass to another member. In those households, your name is replaced by "a former member" in their history.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>See your information and take a copy:</strong> Admins can download a household's data at any time. For anything else, ask us.</li>
        <li><strong>Correct it:</strong> edit it in the app, or ask us.</li>
        <li><strong>Delete it:</strong> delete your account, a household (its owner), or a person (an Admin), or ask us.</li>
        <li><strong>Restrict or object</strong> to how we use it, in some circumstances.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We'll reply within one month. There's no charge.</p>

    <h2>Complaints</h2>
    <p>
        If you're unhappy with how we've used your information, please tell us first at [privacy email address]. We'll acknowledge
        your complaint within 30 days, look into it, and let you know the outcome. You can also complain to the Information
        Commissioner's Office (ICO) at ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint, or on 0303 123 1113.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies and storage on your device</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>A sign-in cookie</strong> keeps you signed in for up to 14 days of inactivity. While you sign in, short-lived
            cookies also protect the sign-in itself. These are strictly necessary, so they don't need your consent.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your device</strong> remembers your light or dark theme choice and a few dismissed tips. The app also keeps its own
            files so it opens quickly and works offline. It never keeps your household's data on your device.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>We don't use analytics or advertising cookies. If that changes, we'll ask first.</p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
        Accounts are for adults (18 and over). A household may record a child, for example a child's part-time job. Only a first name
        or nickname is needed.
    </p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
        Information is sent over encrypted connections and stored in the UK. Access to each household is limited to its members, and
        the app is checked for security problems on every change. If something goes wrong that's likely to put you at risk, we'll
        tell you, and the ICO where required.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
    <p>
        If we change this notice, we'll update the version above. If the change matters, we'll tell you in the app the next time
        you sign in.
    </p>
</LegalPage>
